sion of this conjecture: every locally polyhedral tiling of \\(\\mathbb {R}^3\\) can be completely softened. We also give a short proof of an earlier result of Domokos, G. Horváth, and Regős stating that every suitably nondegenerate polygonic tiling of the plane has, on average, at least two points per cell at which the softness criterion is violated.\n\n## 1 Overview\n\nSpace-filling shapes have long captivated mathematicians; the theory of tilings (or tessellations) has remained a vibrant and actively researched area of geometry from Plato to the present day (Grünbaum and Shephard 1987; Harriss et al. 2017; Schulte 1993). Beyond their architectural and artistic applications, space-filling patterns frequently occur in nature, for example, in the internal structure of crystals and foams. Consequently, the study of tilings has attracted sustained attention across a wide range of natural sciences, with a primary focus on the two- and three-dimensional settings. Polygonal tilings of the plane are defined analogously. Furthermore, a tiling is normal if the sizes of the cells are uniformly bounded, see Definition 1 – this also implies that the number of cells containing any given point is finite and uniformly bounded. We always impose this normality condition. The vertices of the cells are called the nodes of the tiling.\n\nOf particular interest are monohedral tilings, in which all cells are congruent; their common cell is called a monotile. Despite many classical constructions, determining which shapes admit monohedral tilings remains a difficult classification problem. Although numerous space-filling shapes and several infinite families are known, no general classification is available, even within important classes such as tetrahedra. Monotiles satisfying additional geometric requirements are therefore especially noteworthy.\n\nPolyhedral tilings admit various generalizations. Following the work of Domokos, Goriely, G. Horváth, and Regős (Domokos et al. 2024), our focus is on polygonic and polyhedric tilings: here, \\(\\mathbb {R}^d\\) for \\(d=2\\) or \\(d=3\\), respectively, is tiled with not necessarily convex cells that are compact topological d-balls whose pairwise intersections are lower-dimensional topological balls, and which satisfy additional combinatorial and smoothness conditions (see Section 2 for the formal definition). Note that our definition of softness varies from that given in Domokos et al. (2023, 2024); see the remark after Definition 8.\n\nThe first question that arises in the context of soft cells is whether there are completely soft polygonic and polyhedric tilings of \\(\\mathbb {R}^d\\) for \\(d = 2,3\\), respectively. In 2023, Domokos, G. Horváth, and Regős (2023) resolved the planar case, showing that in any polygonic tiling of the plane, the cells must have at least two spikes on average, provided that the average exists. However, the three-dimensional case is markedly different: there exist many polyhedric tilings consisting entirely of soft cells. The focus here is on whether, starting from a polyhedral tiling, one can modify it so that all cells become smooth.\n\nMany polyhedric tilings can be obtained from polyhedral tilings through local diffeomorphisms; however, this is not the case for all (see, e.g., Figure 2). We focus on a particular subclass: a polyhedric tiling is said to be locally polyhedral if every node is locally homeomorphic to a node of a polyhedral tiling (i.e., there is a homeomorphism between suitable neighborhoods of them in the two tilings). However, this local condition does not imply that the entire tiling is globally homeomorphic to a polyhedral tiling (see, e.g., Figure 14).\n\nThe principal question we address is the following: can any locally polyhedral tiling be converted into a completely soft polyhedric tiling by a family of local homeomorphisms? In brief terms, can any locally polyhedral tiling be completely softened? Answering a special case of the question, Domokos, G. Horváth, Goriely, and Regős proved in Domokos et al. (2024) that all polyhedral tilings satisfying a certain combinatorial condition can be completely softened. They further conjectured that this condition is superfluous, namely, that every polyhedral tiling can be completely softened.\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to verify this conjecture for a broader class of tilings. By constructing a new edge-bending algorithm, we prove that every locally polyhedral tiling of \\(\\mathbb {R}^3\\) can be completely softened.\n\n\n### Definition 4\n\nLet V be a node of a polyhedral tiling \\(\\mathcal {M}\\), and select \\(\\varepsilon >0\\) such that \\(B^3(V, \\varepsilon )\\) does not intersect any edge or face of \\(\\mathcal {M}\\) that is not contained in the vertex figure \\(\\nu (V)\\). Then \\(\\nu (V)\\) induces a subdivision of \\(S^2(V,\\varepsilon )\\) whose vertices arise as intersections of the sphere with the edges of \\(\\nu (V)\\), the edges are obtained by taking the intersections with the faces of \\(\\nu (V)\\) (hence these are arcs of great circles), and the faces are given by the intersections of the cells of \\(\\mathcal {M}\\) (thus these are spherically convex domains). We say that the polyhedron P(V) is a vertex polyhedron of V if P(V) is combinatorially equivalent to this subdivision of the sphere \\(S^2(V,\\varepsilon )\\).\n\nWe remark that the induced spherical subdivision is also called the spherical vertex figure. Furthermore, note that the vertex polyhedron is defined only up to combinatorial equivalence. The notion is illustrated in Figure 1b.\n\nThe vertex figure and vertex polyhedron of a node in a cube tiling\n\nNext, adopting the notions from Domokos et al. (2024), we introduce polygonic and polyhedric tilings. Let \\(\\mathcal {M}\\) be a plane or space tiling. We define two functions associated with \\(\\mathcal {M}\\). For a given point \\(p \\in \\mathbb {R}^d\\), let C(p) denote the number of cells in \\(\\mathcal {M}\\) containing p, and let D(p) denote the maximal dimension q such that C is constant in some topological q-ball containing p. Then, we define the type of point p as the pair (C(p), D(p)).\n\nWe start with the planar notion.\n\n### Definition 10\n\nA polyhedric tiling \\(\\mathcal {M}\\) can be completely softened if each of its nodes can be softened using pairwise disjoint bending neighborhoods.\n\nClearly, if a polyhedral tiling can be completely softened, then there exists a soft polyhedric tiling that is combinatorially equivalent to it, thus yielding an answer to the weaker question as well.\n\nOur main focus is the following question posed by Domokos, Goriely, G. Horváth, and Regős (2024): Given a polyhedral tiling of \\(\\mathbb {R}^3\\), does there exist an edge-bending algorithm that yields a complete softening?\n\n### Conjecture 1\n\n(Domokos et al., 2024, Conjecture 1) Every polyhedral tiling can be completely softened.\n\nIn Domokos et al. (2024), the authors proved that Conjecture 1 holds for a wide class of polyhedral tilings.\n\n### Theorem 2\n\n(Domokos et al. 2024) Assume that, for every node V of the polyhedral tiling \\(\\mathcal {M}\\), the vertices of the associated vertex polyhedron P(V) can be partitioned into two classes such that every face contains vertices from both classes and at least one of the two classes is edge-connected. In this case, P(V) is an 11-faced polyhedron that can be realized as the convex hull of the midpoints of the edges of a triangular prism, whose dual edge graph contains no Hamiltonian circuit.\n\nNevertheless, the authors of Domokos et al. (2024) conjectured that every polyhedron admits a vertex partition that satisfies the conditions of Theorem 2. This interesting conjecture, which would yield a proof of Conjecture 1 via their edge-bending algorithm, remains open.\n\nThe main result of the present article establishes the following generalization of Conjecture 1:\n\n### Theorem 3\n\nEvery locally polyhedral tiling can be completely softened.\n\nOur proof relies on a novel edge-bending algorithm that is described in Section 4. By bending all edges of a vertex figure simultaneously, our method avoids the connectedness requirement imposed in Domokos et al. (2024) on one of the two color classes of the corresponding vertex polyhedron.\n\nThe notion of softness can be generalized by requiring higher-dimensional smooth neighborhoods of the boundary points. More precisely, we may define a boundary point p of a cell C to be k-soft if there exists a smoothly embedded topological k-ball in \\(\\partial C\\) whose relative interior contains p. With this terminology, the soft cells considered here are precisely those whose boundary points are at least 1-soft. This convention should not be confused with the codimension-based notion of k-softness recently introduced by Domokos (2026). That framework provides a more systematic treatment of the smooth submanifolds of different dimensions occurring on the boundary of higher-dimensional shapes and cells in tilings.\n\n## 4 Softening Locally Polyhedral Tilings\n\nThe proof of Theorem 3 consists of two main steps. First, we show that a specific combinatorial condition holds for polyhedral graphs. Then, based on this property, we construct a novel edge-bending algorithm.\n\nGiven a polyhedral graph G, a triangulation of it is an augmented graph \\(G_\\Delta \\) that is obtained from G by adding edges (while keeping the same vertex set) so that each face of the resulting graph is a triangle. This can be achieved by subdividing each face of G (including the outer face) into triangles using pairwise non-crossing diagonals (e.g., using all diagonals starting from a given vertex of the face).\n\n### Lemma 2\n\nLet G be a polyhedral graph. Then the vertices of G can be colored with two colors so that there are no monochromatic faces, i.e., every face contains vertices of both color classes.\n\nWe provide two proofs of the above statement.\n\n### First proof of Lemma 2\n\nWe will use a special case of Thomassen’s result on list colorings (Thomassen 2008) stating that, given a planar graph, one can assign one of two colors to each vertex so that no triangle is monochromatic.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputational Geometry: Theory and Applications 129 (2025) 102186Contents lists available at ScienceDirect (https://real.mtak.hu/221003/1/1-s2.0-S0925772125000240-main.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Pach, M. Saghafian and P. SchniderComputational Geometry: Theory and Applications 129 (2025) 102186Fig. ... Suppose that the complete geometric graph K_n can be covered by t plane star-forests, for some positive integer t. ... By the induction hypothesis the edge \\(P_1P_l\\) is supported, so there exists a star-forest \\(F_j\\), \\(j\\neq i\\), which contains \\(P_1P_l\\) along\nJ. Pach, M. Saghafian and P. SchniderComputational Geometry: Theory and Applications 129 (2025) 102186Fig. 2.  In Case 1, recolor \\(P_1P_2,\\dots,P_1P_l\\) from red to blue, and all blue stars spanned by \\(\\{P_2,\\dots,P_l\\}\\) to red.  A dotted line marks the absence of an edge.\n(For interpretation of the colors in the figure(s), the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)Lemma 7. Suppose that the complete geometric graph K_n can be covered by t plane star-forests, for some positive integer t.\nThen, for every k, 1 < k < n, there exists a covering of K_n by t plane star-forests F_1, F_2, …, F_t such that every k′-edge with 1 < k′ ≤ k is supported.Proof. We prove the lemma by induction on $k$.Suppose that \\(k = 2\\). By symmetry, it is sufficient to consider the 2-edge \\(P_1P_3\\) (that is, \\(a = 1\\)). We can assume without\nloss of generality that \\(P_1P_3\\) belongs to \\(F_i\\), for some \\(i\\), and its center is \\(P_1\\) (which implies that \\(P_2P_3\\) is not in \\(F_i\\)). If \\(P_1P_2\\)\nbelongs to \\(F_i\\), condition (i) in Defini