em [15]), etc.\n\nA natural question in this direction is, how much the bound in Theorem 1 can be improved if we drop the assumption that the vertices are in convex position. We conjectured that the above example is optimal, that is, there is no complete geometric graph with n vertices that be decomposed into fewer than plane star-forests. However, recently, Antić, Glišić, and Milivojčević have disproved this conjecture [2].\n\nNote that in the example above, all star-forests had exactly two components. A star-forest consisting of at most k connected components (stars) is said to be a k-star-forest.\n\nIt is also an interesting open problem to determine the minimum number of plane k-star-forests that a complete (convex) geometric graph of n vertices can be decomposed into. We do not even know the answer to the analogous question for abstract graphs.\n\nProblem 2\n\nLet k and n be fixed positive integers. What is the minimum number of k-star-forests that a complete graph {K}_{n} of n vertices can be decomposed into?\n\nAs was mentioned earlier, for , the minimum is . --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposition of Geometric Graphs into Star-Forests* (https://www.renyi.hu/~pach/publications/Covering_with_Plane_TreesGD083023.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: 1.9 years ago; Decomposition of Geometric Graphs into Star-Forests*\n\nJános Pach¹,²[0000−0002−2389−2035], Morteza Saghafian³[0000−0002−4201−5775], and Patrick Schnider⁴[0000−0002−2172−9285] ... Most likely, it was Erdős who first realized that one can ask many interesting new extremal questions for graphs drawn in the plane or in some other surface, if we replace the purely combinatorial conditions by geometric ones; see [11]. ... * János Pach’s Research partially supported by European Research Council (ERC), grant “GeoScape” No. 882971 and by the Hungarian Science Foundation (NKFIH), grant K-131529.\nDecomposition of Geo